
The most compelling thesis narrative I can see from your results so far is "the strength of the LTN constraint matters more than its quantity." You ran with many implication rules and got degenerate solutions; you'll want to show that fewer-but-stronger equivalence rules produce real learning. So when you design the new ontology, deliberately make some experiments minimal — three or four equivalences across all classes — and compare to runs with many more implications. If the equivalence runs win with fewer rules, that's a much stronger story than "we kept adding stuff until something worked."
You might also keep a small set of "pathological" axioms for an appendix experiment — rules that you'd expect to cause collapse — and demonstrate that they reliably do, to show the limitation is general, not specific to your earlier ruleset.

